  {
    "id": "newalphabet",
    "title": "New Alphabet",
    "year": 1775,
    "number": 51,
    "language": "",
    "information": {
      "image": "newalphabet.webp",
      "author": "Thomas Spence (1750-1814) was an English radical, advocating for the common ownership of land and democratic equality between the sexes.",
      "publication": "When he published his alphabet in The Grand Repository of the English Language in 1775, there were numerous socio-linguistic reform movements taking place. The American Revolution had just begun, and a few years earlier, the Industrial Revolution had taken root in England.",
      "motivation": "Given that his political philosophy emphasized equality and unity among the people, it is understandable that he would support the creation of a new, standardized system of English spelling—one that aimed to simplify written language and make it more accessible to all, regardless of education or social class.",
      "system": "He chose to base his system on the Latin alphabet and designed it to use almost exclusively capital letters, giving the script a uniform and bold appearance. For the additional characters, Spence created ligature-like forms by creatively combining existing Latin letters. These new characters were intended for English diphthongs and other sounds that had no clear equivalent in English spelling.",
      "longevity": "The alphabet ultimately failed to gain traction among the general public. Even individuals who supported his political ideals viewed the system as impractical—considering it a needlessly complex and cumbersome method of spelling and writing that offered little advantage over the traditional orthography."
    }
  },
